abstract: >-
  SICA detects intra-session web session hijacking server-side, at constant cost
  per request, by checking a small set of declared session-continuity invariants
  against bounded per-session state. It uses no machine learning and requires no
  attack-labelled training data: invariant weights and the alerting threshold are
  derived from attack-free calibration traffic alone, under a declared
  session-level false-alarm budget. The evaluation uses real public web-server
  access logs as the traffic substrate, with controlled session-hijacking
  injection in which attacker requests are real requests taken verbatim from a
  different real client of the same server. The reported adversary envelope spans
  six masquerade levels, including two co-located cases in which the attacker
  presents the victim's own network address, so that the benchmark measures
  hijack detection rather than address-change detection.

keywords:
  - session hijacking
  - web session security
  - session integrity
  - intrusion detection
  - anomaly detection
  - non-machine-learning detection
  - explainable security
  - web server logs

